README

This sample program uses a Raspberry Pi with two Ethernet ports as EtherCAT
master and shows the status of the EtherCAT network and of the Raspberry Pi
itself on a web page, which is served on the second Ethernet port.

It has been written for the Compute Module 5, which provides one on-board
Ethernet port, extended with a second port using a USB Ethernet device, but
works on any Raspberry Pi model with two available net devices.

	+-------------------+  eth0 (raw EtherCAT frames)  +--------------+
	|  Raspberry Pi     |----------------------------->| EtherCAT     |
	|  (Circle + SOEM)  |                              | slave(s)     |
	|                   |  eth1 (TCP/IP, HTTP)         +--------------+
	|                   |----------------------------->  your browser
	+-------------------+

The EtherCAT master is implemented with SOEM (Simple Open EtherCAT Master) by
the OpenEtherCATsociety. SOEM is not part of Circle and has to be downloaded
before this sample can be built (see below).

Required hardware:

	* A Raspberry Pi with an on-board Ethernet controller (e.g. CM5)
	* One or more EtherCAT slaves, connected to the on-board Ethernet port
	* A USB Ethernet device with a CDC Ethernet (ECM) interface, for
	  instance based on a Realtek RTL815x chip, connected to your network
	  with a DHCP server. On the Raspberry Pi 5 it has to be attached to a
	  root port directly, because USB 3 hubs are not supported.

Do not connect the EtherCAT port to your office network. The EtherCAT master
sends broadcast frames to this port and there is no IP stack behind it.


BUILD

Download and build the SOEM library first, as described in the file
addon/soem/README:

	cd addon/soem
	git clone https://github.com/OpenEtherCATsociety/SOEM.git
	make

Then build this sample as usual:

	cd sample/47-ethercatweb
	make

If the library libsoem.a has not been built, the build of this sample is
skipped with a message, so that a build of all samples does not fail.


WHAT IT DOES

The application creates two independent net device users:

* The on-board Ethernet device (net device 0) is used by the EtherCAT master
  directly. EtherCAT frames are sent to and received from this device without
  a TCP/IP stack in between. The class CEtherCATMaster (see ethercat.h) runs
  the master in an own task:

	- it scans the bus (ecx_config_init()),
	- reads the identity and the CoE objects 0x1008 (device name), 0x1009
	  (hardware version) and 0x100A (software version) from the slaves,
	- maps the process data (ecx_config_map_group()),
	- configures the distributed clocks (ecx_configdc()),
	- brings all slaves to the OPERATIONAL state,
	- exchanges process data every 4 ms (see ETHERCAT_CYCLE_TIME_US in
	  kernel.h) and monitors the working counter,
	- tries to recover lost slaves and scans the bus again, if the
	  connection to all slaves is lost.

  The process data is only read by this sample. If you want to control the
  outputs of a slave, write to the buffer, which is returned in the field
  "outputs" of the group (ctx->grouplist[0].outputs), before the process data
  is sent in CEtherCATMaster::CyclicProcess().

* The second Ethernet device (net device 1) runs a TCP/IP stack, which gets
  its IP address via DHCP. The web server (see webserver.h) serves a dashboard
  on port 80, which is updated once per second from the URL "/status.json".
  The IP address to be used is written to the system log.

The web page shows:

	* the state of the EtherCAT master and of the Ethernet link,
	* the number of slaves, the working counter and the cycle counters,
	* the frame statistics of the EtherCAT net device,
	* the process data (first 16 bytes per direction),
	* a table with all slaves (state, AL status code, addresses, identity,
	  process data sizes and the parameters read via CoE),
	* the status of the Raspberry Pi: model, SoC, RAM size, CPU clock rate,
	  SoC temperature, throttled state, free heap space and uptime,
	* the network configuration and the state of all net devices,
	* the tasks of the Circle scheduler.

The system log shows the state of the master every 10 seconds too.


CONFIGURATION

The used net devices and the cycle time are defined in kernel.h:

	ETHERCAT_NET_DEVICE	net device for EtherCAT (default 0)
	WEBSERVER_NET_DEVICE	net device for the web server (default 1)
	ETHERCAT_CYCLE_TIME_US	process data cycle time (default 4000 us)

The on-board Ethernet device is initialized before the USB host controller in
CKernel::Initialize(), so that it becomes net device 0 and the USB Ethernet
device becomes net device 1. See doc/multiple-net-devices.txt for details.

The number of slaves, which is shown on the web page, and the size of the
process data image are defined in ethercat.h (ETHERCAT_MAX_SLAVES and
ETHERCAT_IOMAP_SIZE). The maximum number of slaves on the bus is defined as
EC_MAXSLAVE in addon/soem/include/soem/ec_options.h.


NOTES

The scheduler of Circle is cooperative and non-preemptive. The EtherCAT task
does not give control to other tasks, while it waits for a frame to return
(up to EC_TIMEOUTRET, 2 ms). Cycle times below one millisecond are not
recommended. There is no synchronization of the system time of the Raspberry
Pi to the distributed clock of the EtherCAT network.

If no slaves are found or the Ethernet link is down, the master scans the bus
again every five seconds. The web server works in this case too and shows the
reason in the field "Last error".
